# user/wsysd.ad — the hamnix-linux scene compositor.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# ============
# In Hamnix the compositor is IN THE KERNEL: sys/src/9/port/devwsys.ad both
# holds the window table and z-blits every window's rasterized scene to the
# framebuffer. On Linux the window table stays "kernel-side" (shared memory
# behind /dev/wsys — user/linux-wsys.c) but the RASTERIZER moves out here,
# into an ordinary Adder program. That is the shape the north star asks for:
# the boot process and the userland are Adder; the Linux kernel is only the
# thing underneath.
#
# It needs no private syscall. Everything it does, any program could do:
#
#   read  /dev/fb              -> "W H PITCH BPP PIXFMT\n", the screen
#   write /dev/wsys/ctl        -> "screen W H", so clients can size themselves
#   read  /dev/wsys            -> the wid set (a directory listing)
#   read  /dev/wsys/<wid>/ctl  -> "<wid> <x> <y> <w> <h> <z> <dec> <vis>
#                                  <proto> <gen>", the window's state
#   read  /dev/wsys/<wid>/scene-> the display list, published whole by `commit`
#   write /dev/wsys/<wid>/keys -> route a key to the focused window
#   write /dev/fb              -> present
#
# THE RASTERIZER IS NOT NEW CODE
# ------------------------------
# lib/hamui_host.ad already parses the lib/hamscene.ad display-list grammar
# and draws it with the vk 2D primitive ops — it was written as the HOST sink
# so a GUI app's render could be looked at without booting. It is pure Adder
# with no syscalls, so it links here unchanged. The compositor asks it to
# rasterize each window into its RGBA image, then blits that image into the
# screen composite at the window's position, in z order.
#
# WHY A COMPOSITE BUFFER AND NOT DIRECT-TO-FB
# -------------------------------------------
# Windows overlap. Painting them straight to /dev/fb in z order would show
# each one being drawn (and would flush the display once per window). We
# compose off-screen and write the screen once per frame, which is both
# atomic to the eye and one flush.
#
# ====================================================================
# WHERE A FRAME ACTUALLY GOES — AND WHY THAT DECIDED THE GPU QUESTION
# ====================================================================
# `wsysd -bench N` (below) measures it. Offscreen at 1280x800, HAMFB_FILE,
# with a full-screen backdrop plus one 320x200 decorated app — the shape of a
# real session — the frame used to break down like this, in microseconds:
#
#     total 5503 | clear 1049 | scene 15 | rast 618 | copy 3444 | present 358
#
# Read that again before designing anything with the word "Vulkan" in it. The
# vk2d RASTERIZATION — every fill, blit, glyph and rounded rect in the whole
# desktop, the only thing a GPU rasterizer would replace — is 618 us, ELEVEN
# PERCENT of the frame. The two dumbest lines in this file, a full-screen fill
# done one bounds-checked pixel at a time and a window copy-out done one
# function call at a time, were 4493 us, EIGHTY-TWO PERCENT. A GPU that made
# rasterization instantaneous would have taken the frame from 5.5 ms to 4.9 ms.
#
# So the honest first step was not a GPU. It was:
#   * fill_rect: clip once, store words                       (1049 -> 0 us,
#     and 0 rather than ~250 because a full-screen backdrop makes the desktop
#     clear pure waste — screen_is_covered() skips it)
#   * the copy-out: render in the screen's byte order (vk2d already had
#     vk2d_set_bgra, for the virtio-gpu scanout) and copy rows as words
#                                                             (3444 -> 545 us)
#   * a full-screen window rasterizes STRAIGHT INTO the composite, so there is
#     no copy and no separate clear at all         (545 -> 33 us, rast absorbs
#     the clear it was doing anyway)
#   * the cursor gets a save-under, so a mouse move touches two 12x17 rects
#     instead of re-rasterizing the desktop        (6543 -> 8 us, ~800x)
#
# Measured after, same fixture, pixel-for-pixel identical output (0 differing
# RGB pixels over 60 full frames and 60 cursor moves):
#
#     total  730 | clear 0 | scene 9 | rast 496 | copy 32 | present 186
#     cursor-only frame: 8 us
#
# 7.5x on a repaint, ~800x on a pointer move. Rasterization is now 68% of what
# is left, which is the point at which a GPU rasterizer would be worth having —
# it was not before.
#
# THE GPU, NOW THAT THERE IS ONE — AND THE GATE THAT DECIDES
# ----------------------------------------------------------
# The two obstacles this comment used to list are both gone. Commit 8c402a9d
# installs the Vulkan userspace (loader + venus/ANV/NVK/RADV/lavapipe ICDs +
# libdrm) into the HAMNIX root through hpm, so there is an ICD to dlopen from
# OUT here and no Debian namespace in the picture; and commit c86af3ab is a
# real Vulkan backend for the vk spine (lib/vk/vk_linux.ad + user/linux-vk.c),
# byte-identical to lib/vk/vk_2d.ad and gated as such.
#
# So this file now selects it — and the interesting part is that it must NOT
# select it unconditionally:
#
#   MEASURED, lavapipe (the CPU software ICD), 1280x800: the GPU path is 2.3x
#   SLOWER on fills and 2.9x slower with glyph runs. That is not a defect. On
#   a CPU ICD the same CPU runs the same integer math through JIT'd generic
#   SPIR-V, against vk_2d's hand-tuned packed-word loops. Arming it there
#   would make the FALLBACK configuration — the one a machine with no GPU
#   runs — three times slower, which is the exact shape of failure this tree
#   keeps hitting: something that answers success-shaped instead of true.
#
# The gate is therefore: arm on real SILICON (VkPhysicalDeviceType != 4),
# stay software on a CPU-type device, and say WHICH and WHY in one line at
# startup. HAMNIX_WSYSD_VK=1 forces it on (that is how the GPU path is
# exercised on a host that has no GPU to give); HAMNIX_WSYSD_VK=0 forces it
# off (that is how a machine with a GPU that turns out to be slower, or a
# driver that misbehaves, is recovered without a rebuild).
#
# ZERO COPY, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE SAME THING AS FAST.
# vk_linux_frame_alloc() returns the mapped address of the GPU frame, and THE
# COMPOSITE IS BOUND TO IT (comp_base, below). So a routed frame costs no
# upload and no readback: the shader writes the same bytes present() then hands
# to /dev/fb.
#
# That binding was measured 13x SLOWER than the software rasterizer, and the
# reason was WHERE the frame lived, not that it was shared. It was allocated
# DEVICE_LOCAL, which on a discrete card with no resizable BAR is the PCIe BAR
# aperture: host-mappable, so nothing failed or warned, and uncached, so the
# CPU read it at 0.05 GB/s. present_rows() write(2)s the whole composite to
# /dev/fb and cursor_save_under() reads it directly -- a compositor reads its
# own composite EVERY frame -- so 0.16 ms of copy became 67 ms of bus.
#
# The frame is now placed in HOST_CACHED memory. The shader's writes cross
# PCIe instead, which is the trade, and it is a good one because it is not
# symmetric: a GPU write across PCIe is POSTED and the device does not stall on
# it, while a CPU read of VRAM is a synchronous round trip. Measured on an
# RTX 3090, the same 4 MiB copy-out: 88.0 ms device-local, 0.22 ms host-cached.
# The startup line says which one this machine got; the router in lib/vk/vk_2d.ad
# is armed only for that one surface, which is why the full-screen backdrop
# (the window that rasterizes STRAIGHT into the composite, and the bulk of
# the pixels in a real session) is what reaches the device, while a small
# window's private image stays on the CPU where it already was.
#
# ADDER QUIRKS HONOURED: big buffers are top-level BSS arrays, no adjacent
# string-literal concatenation, string literals are passed at the call site.

from lib.hamscene import hamscene_load, hamscene_begin, hamscene_glyphs
